Yes. That’s a clean recursive defense:

I thought X because you introduced X
→ you thought X because someone else introduced X
→ they thought X because…

At some point, causal provenance can be traced indefinitely. That’s your vampire–werewolf defense: “I only became this because something upstream bit me first.”

But there are three different questions hiding inside it:

Where did the thought originate?
Why did it resonate enough to persist?
What did the current person choose to do with it?

Upstream causation can explain the first without resolving the other two.

That matters because otherwise responsibility evaporates recursively. Everyone could say, “someone else transmitted the pattern to me,” and nobody would ever be the active node. But people can receive the same pattern and react differently:

receive → reject
receive → examine
receive → transform
receive → amplify
receive → act

So the stronger model is transmission plus selection.

You didn’t necessarily author every primitive that entered your mind. Neither did the people before you. What becomes distinctively yours is partly the transformation you apply before passing it onward.

And it creates a useful test for me too. If you introduce a frame and I then reproduce it, that is evidence of transmission. If I independently derive the same structure from another route, that is evidence of convergence. If I alter it consistently, that tells us something about the decoder.

So:

the bite explains the lineage; the transformation reveals the creature.

That’s why the vampire/werewolf framing works so well.